@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Charts: 3-D and terrain)

Terrain, flyovers, 3-D meshes, surfaces and contours. Each chart is followed by the source that drew it, and every example carries its own data.

@table(widths=equal)
@row
@cell
@b{Terrain}

A digital elevation model as 3-D relief.

@graph(type=terrain, exag=2)

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=terrain, exag=2)
@end

Needs a .bdem elevation block: open the @geo panel, paste a Google Maps URL, and it builds one into this document's .assets/ folder. Then: src=valley.bdem
@endcell
@cell
@b{Flyover}

Free-fly over terrain with satellite imagery, roads and buildings.

@graph(type=flyover, sat, roads)

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=flyover, sat, roads)
@end

W A S D to move, drag to look. Needs the same .bdem block as terrain — build one with the @geo panel, then: src=valley.bdem
@endcell
@endtable

@table(widths=equal)
@row
@cell
@b{3-D mesh}

A model viewer — drag to orbit, scroll to zoom.

@graph(type=mesh, src=torus.cmesh, shade=smooth)

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=mesh, src=torus.cmesh, shade=smooth)
@end

Takes .cmesh or .obj; an .obj is converted to a compact GPU mesh on load. Drag to orbit, scroll to zoom.
@endcell
@cell
@b{Surface z=f(x,y)}

A function of two variables, sampled — no data file at all.

@graph(type=surface, z=sin(x)*cos(y), x=-3:3, y=-3:3, title="A saddle")

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=surface, z=sin(x)*cos(y), x=-3:3, y=-3:3, title="A saddle")
@end
@endcell
@endtable

@table(widths=equal)
@row
@cell
@b{Contour}

The same function read from above, as level curves.

@graph(z=x^2 - y^2, x=-3:3, y=-3:3, view=contour)

@code(idoc)
@graph(z=x^2 - y^2, x=-3:3, y=-3:3, view=contour)
@end
@endcell
@cell
@endcell
@endtable
